# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5.memetic-wikitext" "A sigil names its own verb, and a bearing arrow survives inside one"

# THE DIALECT NAMES NOTHING FOR THE THING IT EXISTS TO SAY. TiddlyWiki reads `<<~ set …>>` as a call
# on a variable spelled `~`, so the verb binds as a POSITIONAL argument and paints exactly like any
# unquoted value a carrier writes. `set`, `oracle`, `node` and `stage` all read `string.unquoted`.
#
# The base grammar must keep its reading — the host builds a transclusion here and nothing else —
# so the dialect names the verb BESIDE what the base says rather than instead of it. Every value of a
# call rides NAMED and TICKED (the kupono grammar), so the base names each key an attribute.

<<~ set lares=`aim` mode=`scan`>>
#   ^^^ entity.name.function.sigil.memetic-wikitext
#       ^^^^^ entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5

<<~ oracle throw=`a reading`>>
#   ^^^^^^ entity.name.function.sigil.memetic-wikitext
#          ^^^^^ entity.other.attribute-name.argument.tiddlywiki5

# A CLOSER NAMES THE SIGIL IT CLOSES. TiddlyWiki reads `~/ahu` as one variable name, so the base
# offers a reader nothing to tell a closing sigil from an opening one.

<<~/ahu>>
#   ^^^ entity.name.function.sigil.memetic-wikitext

# THE BEARING ARROW LEAVES THE SIGIL. A call carries its ends as named values (`from=` and `to=`),
# so no arrow stands between two values, and an arrow INSIDE a ticked value — a gauge's slide, a
# ladder — is that value's text and wears the string's own name, never the arrow's.

<<~ set lares=`yield` aperture=`10/measure -> 12/measure` to=`?`>>
#                                          ^^ string.template.substituted.single.attribute.html.tiddlywiki5
#                                          ^^ - keyword.operator.bearing.arrow.memetic-wikitext

# THE ARROW STAYS IN PROSE. The markers a turn writes between phases fuse a phase glyph onto the
# arrow (`->✶ ->⏿ ->◇ ->▶ ->↺`), and a bare arrow between words still reads.

A phase marker ->✶ stands named in prose, and so does a bare -> between words.
#              ^^ keyword.operator.bearing.arrow.memetic-wikitext
#                                                            ^^ keyword.operator.bearing.arrow.memetic-wikitext
